\section{Objective}\label{objective}

The configuration originates from the 1990s on UNIX systems, and many
parts are deprecated, commented out or redundant. Audit
\texttt{site-pkgs.org} and produce actionable modernisation guidance.

\subsection{Operational definitions}\label{operational-definitions}

\begin{itemize}
\tightlist
\item
  \emph{Obsolete} means one of the following:

  \begin{itemize}
  \tightlist
  \item
    The package is archived, unmaintained or no longer on its archive.
  \item
    A built-in feature in Emacs 32.0.50 supersedes it (for example
    \texttt{use-package}, \texttt{eglot}, \texttt{project.el},
    tree-sitter modes, \texttt{which-key}, \texttt{use-short-answers}).
  \item
    A modern replacement is clearly preferred (for example Helm,
    Ivy/Counsel/Swiper, Company, Projectile and \texttt{ido} are
    candidates for replacement by vertico, consult, corfu and
    \texttt{project.el}).
  \item
    The code uses a function or variable that is deprecated or obsolete.
  \end{itemize}
\item
  \emph{Unused} means any of the following:

  \begin{itemize}
  \tightlist
  \item
    The block is commented out, \texttt{:disabled}, or marked
    \texttt{:tangle\ no}.
  \item
    The package is installed but never configured, bound or hooked.
  \item
    It is dead code (for example a branch for old Emacs versions).
  \end{itemize}
\item
  \emph{Duplicate or conflicting} means two or more active features that
  provide the same function. Examples include multiple completion
  frameworks, the same key bound twice, overlapping linters or
  formatters, flymake versus flycheck, or multiple
  \texttt{completion-at-point} sources fighting each other.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Target design}\label{target-design}

\begin{itemize}
\tightlist
\item
  Preferred completion stack: vertico, orderless, marginalia, consult,
  embark (and \texttt{embark-consult}) for the minibuffer, and corfu
  with cape for in-buffer completion. Recommend additions or removals
  only with reasons.
\item
  Both LSP clients must remain.

  \begin{itemize}
  \tightlist
  \item
    \texttt{lsp-mode} is assumed for ordinary Python code. If the file
    shows a different LSP client, report that.
  \item
    \texttt{eglot} handles percent-format Jupyter notebooks (Python
    files with \texttt{\#\ \%\%} cell markers).
  \item
    Both clients operate on \texttt{.py} files in the same major mode,
    so propose a deterministic per-buffer mechanism that guarantees
    exactly one client per buffer. Consider the \texttt{\#\ \%\%}
    marker, a directory-local variable and a hook. Notebooks are always
    stored in \texttt{notebooks} subdirectories of base project folder.
    Also check how diagnostics (flymake versus flycheck),
    \texttt{completion-at-point}, formatting (\texttt{ruff}), eldoc,
    xref and key prefixes interact in each case.
  \item
    Report which client, if any, is configured for R and Julia.
  \end{itemize}
\item
  \texttt{eshell} and \texttt{tramp} are out of scope for this pass.
  List any related findings under "Deferred" without analysing them.
\end{itemize}

