\section{Executive summary}\label{executive-summary}

The file defines 380 \texttt{use-package} forms: 212 active, 58
\texttt{:disabled} and 110 commented out. No source block uses
\texttt{:tangle\ no}, so every block is tangled. Roughly 45\% of the
forms are therefore dead weight. Nested forms inside disabled parents
are counted as active by the scan, so the real active count is slightly
lower.

The most damaging problems are functional, not cosmetic:

\begin{itemize}
\tightlist
\item
  The Python lsp-mode/Eglot switch cannot work as written. Four
  independent defects were found (DUP-03 to DUP-06). The notebook hook
  is mis-nested, \texttt{derived-mode-p\ \textquotesingle{}python-mode}
  rejects tree-sitter buffers, the \texttt{remove-hook} calls target
  functions that were never added, and the Eglot server command
  (\texttt{rass}, \texttt{ty}) is not in the declared toolchain.
\item
  \texttt{(global-company-mode\ nil)} enables Company globally, because
  a nil argument means \emph{enable} when called from Lisp. Company and
  Corfu then compete in every buffer (DUP-01).
\item
  Several keys point at packages that are disabled or never installed
  (\texttt{vimish-fold}, \texttt{helm-buffers-list}, \texttt{ranger}),
  so they fail on use (DUP-10).
\item
  The Python \texttt{:custom} form contains a \texttt{cond}, so the
  interpreter choice is never applied (ENH-05).
\end{itemize}

The target stack is mostly in place: vertico, orderless, marginalia,
consult, embark, embark-consult, corfu and cape are all configured. What
remains is removing the legacy layer, fixing the defects above, and
adding a deterministic per-buffer LSP selector (ENH-01).

Verification limits: only \texttt{site-pkgs.org} and the keymap scan
were provided. Anything that depends on other \texttt{site-*.el} files
is marked \emph{not verifiable from the provided files}. No browsing was
done, so the GitHub repository was not compared with the attachment.

