\section{\texorpdfstring{\textbf{1. Executive
summary}}{1. Executive summary}}\label{1-executive-summary}

The audit uses the attached \texttt{site-pkgs.org} as its authoritative
source, with \texttt{emacs-keymap-scan.txt} as supplementary evidence
for global keybindings. The target version is Emacs \texttt{32.0.50},
not the latest stable release.

The configuration is not simply an old configuration requiring wholesale
replacement. It already contains a substantial modern foundation:

\begin{itemize}
\tightlist
\item
  A modern minibuffer stack: Vertico, Orderless, Marginalia, Consult and
  Embark.
\item
  Corfu and Cape for in-buffer completion.
\item
  Both \texttt{lsp-mode} and built-in Eglot.
\item
  \texttt{project.el}, alongside Projectile and Consult integrations.
\item
  Ruff formatting and basedpyright for Python.
\item
  Tree-sitter integration.
\item
  Modern Org facilities, including Denote and Citar.
\end{itemize}

The main problems are inconsistent activation policies, legacy
configurations retained alongside modern ones, duplicated project and
completion functionality, and some implementation errors in the Python
LSP switching mechanism.

\textbf{The most important recommendation is to retain both LSP clients
but make backend selection deterministic before either client starts.}
The current notebook selection code does not reliably achieve that.

