project-management
framework}}{OBS-02: Projectile as the primary project-management framework}}\label{obs-02-projectile-as-the-primary-project-management-framework}

\begin{itemize}
\tightlist
\item
  Location: org heading \texttt{Workspace}, subsection \texttt{Project}
\item
  Status: obsolete as the primary project framework; partially active
\item
  Evidence:

  \begin{itemize}
  \tightlist
  \item
    \texttt{projectile} is configured.
  \item
    \texttt{projectile-global-mode} is commented out.
  \item
    \texttt{consult-projectile}, \texttt{treemacs-projectile} and
    Projectile-specific Hydra commands remain configured.
  \item
    The built-in \texttt{project.el} is already used by Consult through
    \texttt{consult-project-buffer}.
  \item
    Dashboard explicitly selects Projectile as its project backend.
  \end{itemize}
\item
  Recommendation:

  \begin{itemize}
  \tightlist
  \item
    Make \texttt{project.el} the canonical project abstraction.
  \item
    Replace Projectile-specific commands with built-in project commands
    and Consult.
  \item
    Retain Treemacs independently if its file-tree interface is useful.
  \item
    Remove \texttt{consult-projectile} after migrating its bindings.
  \item
    Avoid enabling Projectile globally merely to preserve legacy
    integrations.
  \end{itemize}
\item
  Risk: Medium; Effort: Medium; Confidence: High
\end{itemize}

\texttt{project.el} is built into the target Emacs distribution. It
provides project discovery, project switching, file finding and project
buffers without requiring Projectile.\footnote{\href{https://www.gnu.org/software/emacs/manual/html_node/emacs/Projects.html?utm_source=chatgpt.com}{GNU
