  suggests dead or competing keymaps. Treat \texttt{site-load.el} and
  \texttt{site-init.el} as dead code: they are not loaded by
  \texttt{site-boot.el} (which loads \texttt{site-pref},
  \texttt{site-conf}, \texttt{site-pkgs}, \texttt{site-func},
  \texttt{site-cust}, \texttt{site-misc}, \texttt{site-edit},
  \texttt{site-view}, \texttt{site-keys}, \texttt{site-tool},
  \texttt{site-user}).
\item
  \textbf{The notebook hook syntax error.} The final \texttt{add-hook}
  in \texttt{Lang:\ Python/lsp} is missing a closing parenthesis in the
  \texttt{and} form:

\begin{Shaded}
\begin{Highlighting}[]
\NormalTok{(when (and buffer{-}file{-}name }
\NormalTok{           (string{-}match{-}p "notebooks" (buffer{-}file{-}name))}
\NormalTok{  ;; \^{} missing one closing paren for \textasciigrave{}and\textasciigrave{}}
\end{Highlighting}
\end{Shaded}

  This will signal \texttt{wrong-type-argument} when the hook runs.
  ENH‑01 replaces it.
\item
  \textbf{R has no LSP client configured.} \texttt{ess} is configured
  for R, but neither \texttt{lsp-mode} nor \texttt{eglot} is hooked into
  \texttt{ess-mode} or \texttt{R-mode}. If R LSP is desired, add a hook
  for \texttt{ess-r-mode} or \texttt{R-mode}. Currently, R relies on
  ESS’s built‑in completion and help, which is acceptable but should be
  an explicit choice.
\item
  \textbf{Julia has two LSP clients configured} (\texttt{eglot-jl} and
  \texttt{lsp-julia}). This is a conflict (DUP‑03). Choose one and
  disable the other.
\item
  \textbf{\texttt{use-package} is built‑in} in Emacs 32, but the
  configuration still contains commented‑out bootstrap code (Straight,
  Quelpa, manual \texttt{package-install} of \texttt{use-package}) under
  \texttt{Starting\ up\ →\ Use-Package}. That code is dead (commented
