\subsection{LSP/Eglot Coexistence
Design}\label{lspeglot-coexistence-design}

Both clients are required: lsp-mode for ordinary Python, eglot for
\texttt{\#\ \%\%} notebooks. The file already contains the right
\emph{parts} — a buffer-local flag \texttt{h7/python-use-eglot}, a
switch function \texttt{h7/python-lsp-switch}, and guard lambdas on the
lsp-mode hooks — but the activation path has four defects:

\begin{enumerate}
\def\labelenumi{\arabic{enumi}.}
\tightlist
\item
  Broken guard: \texttt{h7/python-lsp-switch} checks
  \texttt{(derived-mode-p\ \textquotesingle{}python-mode)}. Since Emacs
  29, \texttt{python-ts-mode} derives from \texttt{python-base-mode},
  not \texttt{python-mode}, so the guard signals \texttt{user-error} in
  exactly the \texttt{python-ts-mode} notebook buffers the feature
  targets (needs verification against your exact build; High confidence
  on the parent-mode change).
\item
  Non-deterministic detection: the auto hook matches the substring
  \texttt{"notebooks"} anywhere in the path — it fires for
  \texttt{notebooks-backup/}, any file named \texttt{*notebooks*}, and
  non-notebook files inside such directories, and never checks the
  \texttt{\#\ \%\%} marker that defines the actual format.
\item
  Ineffective \texttt{remove-hook} calls: removing a hook that was added
  as an anonymous lambda by \texttt{use-package\ :hook} cannot match
  (lambda identity); those two lines are no-ops.
\item
  Eglot is registered for \texttt{python-ts-mode} only; a notebook
  opened in \texttt{python-mode} has no server program.
\end{enumerate}

The replacement design (ENH-02) makes the decision deterministic: a
single router on \texttt{python-base-mode-hook}, driven by (a) an
explicit buffer-local/directory-local flag, (b) the \texttt{notebooks/}
directory component, or (c) a \texttt{\#\ \%\%} marker scan in the first
200 lines. Interaction matrix after the fix:

\begin{itemize}
\tightlist
\item
  Diagnostics: flycheck in lsp-mode buffers (unchanged), flymake in
  eglot buffers; \texttt{consult-lsp} vs \texttt{consult-flymake} chosen
  by client; no checker duplication after ENH-05.
\item
  Completion-at-point: both clients provide a CAPF; \texttt{C-SPC}
  (site-keys) and corfu work identically; cape remains the extension
  point.
\item
  Formatting: \texttt{ruff-format-on-save-mode} stays lsp-mode-only;
  notebooks rely on eglot (ruff is in the eglot server set), optionally
  \texttt{eglot-format-on-save}.
\item
  Eldoc: lsp-ui-doc (lsp-mode) vs \texttt{eldoc-doc-buffer} on
  \texttt{C-h\ .} (eglot) — unchanged, matches the REFS table.
\item
  Xref: both clients plug into xref; the global \texttt{consult-xref}
  configuration serves both.
\item
  Key prefixes: see ENH-10 (unify under \texttt{C-l}).
\end{itemize}

R and Julia: Julia = eglot via eglot-jl (active; remove lsp-julia,
OBS-15). R has \emph{no} LSP client configured — ESS only. Optional:
eglot for R using the REditorSupport language server, consistent with
the Julia choice (ENH-15, needs verification of server availability
inside renv containers).

