\subsubsection{DUP-03: lsp-mode and eglot per-buffer switching is broken
in four
places}\label{dup-03-lsp-mode-and-eglot-per-buffer-switching-is-broken-in-four-places}

\begin{itemize}
\item
  Location: org headings "Lang: LSP.mode" (\texttt{:hook} entries),
  "Lang: Python/mode" (\texttt{:hook} reset), "Lang: Python/lsp"
  (\texttt{h7/python-lsp-switch},
  \texttt{defvar-local\ h7/python-use-eglot}, final \texttt{add-hook}),
  "Lang: Python/lsp" (\texttt{eglot-server-programs})
\item
  Status: conflicting and defective; the requirement is exactly one
  client per buffer, and the current code can start both
\item
  Evidence, item by item:

  \begin{itemize}
  \tightlist
  \item
    Wrong mode predicate: \texttt{h7/python-lsp-switch} begins with
    \texttt{(unless\ (derived-mode-p\ \textquotesingle{}python-mode)\ (user-error\ ...))}.
    In a \texttt{python-ts-mode} buffer this predicate is false because
    \texttt{python-ts-mode} derives from \texttt{python-base-mode}, not
    \texttt{python-mode}. The automatic notebook hook runs in
    \texttt{python-ts-mode}, so the switcher errors out there.
  \item
    Paren misplacement in the auto-eglot hook: the
    \texttt{(when\ (and\ buffer-file-name\ (string-match-p\ "notebooks"\ (buffer-file-name))}
    form is never closed before the body, so the \texttt{remove-hook}
    calls and \texttt{h7/python-use-eglot} become additional
    \texttt{and} arguments. It happens to work by accident, but it is
    not the intended structure.
  \item
    Hook-ordering race: hooks run in reverse order of addition. The
    \texttt{python} use-package form (line 4992) adds a hook that sets
    \texttt{h7/python-use-eglot} to nil; the lsp-mode form (line 4504)
    adds the \texttt{lsp-deferred} hook; the auto-eglot lambda (line
    5400) is added last and therefore runs first. In a notebook buffer
    the sequence is: eglot starts, then the nil-setter resets the flag,
    then the lsp hook sees nil and calls \texttt{lsp-deferred}, so both
    clients attach to the same buffer.
  \item
    Useless remove-hook calls:
    \texttt{(remove-hook\ \textquotesingle{}python-mode-hook\ \#\textquotesingle{}lsp-deferred\ t)}
    removes nothing, because the hook contains the use-package lambda,
    not the bare symbol \texttt{lsp-deferred}, and the local flag is
    meaningless for a globally added hook.
  \item
    Broken server spec (also ENH-04):
    \texttt{(add-to-list\ \textquotesingle{}eglot-server-programs\ \textquotesingle{}(python-ts-mode\ .\ ("rass"\ "-\/-"\ "ty"\ "server"\ "-\/-"\ "ruff"\ "server")))}.
    \texttt{rass} is not an executable (evidently a typo for
    \texttt{basedpyright-langserver}), and the \texttt{-\/-} separators
    are literal arguments, not eglot syntax. The eglot multi-server form
    is a list of complete command strings \footnote{Eglot manual, GNU
      Emacs,
      \url{https://www.gnu.org/software/emacs/manual/html_node/eglot/}}.
  \end{itemize}
\item
  Recommendation: replace the three hook fragments with one dispatcher
  (ENH-05) and fix the server spec (ENH-04). Both clients stay: lsp-mode
  for ordinary Python, eglot with basedpyright plus ruff for
  percent-format notebooks under \texttt{notebooks/} directories
\item
  Risk: High; Effort: Medium; Confidence: High
\end{itemize}

