\subsection{LSP/eglot coexistence design
(summary)}\label{lspeglot-coexistence-design-summary}

The deterministic mechanism that satisfies the requirement is:

\begin{enumerate}
\def\labelenumi{\arabic{enumi}.}
\tightlist
\item
  Exactly one entry point: a single \texttt{python-base-mode-hook}
  dispatcher decides the client before either package hook runs
  (ENH-05).
\item
  The decision input is a directory-local variable set in each
  \texttt{notebooks/} subdirectory, with the path check as a fallback.
  Directory-locals are the durable, per-project override; the
  \texttt{notebooks} name match covers projects without a
  \texttt{.dir-locals.el}.
\item
  Diagnostics: lsp-mode buffers use flycheck
  (\texttt{lsp-diagnostics-provider\ :flycheck}); eglot buffers use
  flymake, with flycheck suppressed per buffer (ENH-06).
\item
  Completion: both clients feed \texttt{completion-at-point}, so corfu
  works unchanged in both cases; no cape fallbacks are needed in Python
  buffers.
\item
  Formatting: \texttt{ruff-format-on-save-mode} stays on for lsp-mode
  buffers and is disabled by the dispatcher for eglot buffers, because
  the eglot session already runs \texttt{ruff\ server} \footnote{Ruff
    documentation, \url{https://docs.astral.sh/ruff/}}.
\item
  Eldoc and xref: both clients provide them through the standard hooks;
  no special casing needed.
\item
  Key prefixes: lsp-mode keeps \texttt{C-l} (see ENH-08) and eglot keeps
  \texttt{C-c\ c\ a} / \texttt{C-c\ c\ r}; the dispatcher bindings
  \texttt{C-c\ l\ e} / \texttt{C-c\ l\ l} remain as manual overrides.
\item
  R and Julia: R has no LSP client configured (ESS only, org heading
  "Lang: R/ess"); Julia uses eglot via \texttt{eglot-jl} with
  \texttt{lsp-julia} loaded but inert (its hook is commented out). If
  lsp-mode should own Julia instead, that is a separate decision; today
  eglot wins there.
\end{enumerate}

