\section{6. Additional notes}\label{6-additional-notes-1}

\begin{itemize}
\tightlist
\item
  ghostel: the file treats ghostel as a terminal emulator package with a
  vterm-like API (\texttt{ghostel-send-key},
  \texttt{ghostel-download-module}, \texttt{INSIDE\_EMACS\ =\ ghostel}).
  I could not verify such a package on MELPA from the provided material;
  if it is an internal rename of emacs-libvterm \footnote{emacs-libvterm,
    \url{https://github.com/akermu/emacs-libvterm}}, say so in a comment
  so future audits do not treat it as unknown. Confidence: Low, needs
  verification.
\item
  evil: \texttt{use-package\ evil} is active with \texttt{:defer\ 30}
  but nothing in this file ever calls \texttt{evil-mode}. The
  \texttt{:general\ :states} blocks (helpful, visual-regexp) and
  \texttt{ha-leader} only make sense with evil active, so it is
  presumably enabled in \texttt{site-edit.el} or \texttt{site-keys.el};
  not verifiable from the provided files. If evil is not actually
  enabled, those general blocks are inert.
\item
  ob-async: active in the "Org babel" heading; modern Org has native
  asynchronous babel execution and ob-async has known conflicts with
  newer Org. Needs verification, Low confidence.
\item
  auctex-latexmk: active; upstream has been quiet for years while AUCTeX
  gained its own latexmk support. Needs verification, Low confidence.
\item
  \texttt{python-pytest} bindings use the \texttt{C-c\ t} prefix; the
  in-file comment about treemacs on \texttt{C-c\ t} is stale (treemacs
  is on \texttt{C-c\ f}).
\item
  \texttt{org-element-use-cache\ nil} is a performance workaround for a
  cache error; revisit on the Emacs 32 build since the underlying
  org-element cache bugs have seen fixes.
\item
  The REFS tables at the top ("GLOBAL", "Buffers") document \texttt{C-.}
  as \texttt{embark-act} and \texttt{C-h\ B} as
  \texttt{embark-bindings}, which match the embark block; keep them in
  sync when bindings change (the \texttt{C-c\ h} row already drifted).
\item
  Verification statement: every finding above cites an org heading or
  package name present in the attached \texttt{site-pkgs.org}, and no
  recommendation removes or contradicts the requirement that lsp-mode
  and eglot both remain, with exactly one client per Python buffer.
\end{itemize}

